POINT 2a -- THE CLAIM I WAS ASKED TO SCRUTINISE: is the blocking machinery
  necessary, or would a simpler test have discriminated? The worker is RIGHT,
  and I established it by construction rather than by reading its argument.
  I wrote the naive post-hoc probe it says cannot work (zz_probe_test.go:
  seed the cache via s.ReadFile, call rollbackDecryptionWithRecipient, inspect
  s.cache AFTER the call returns) and ran it on the correct tree and on mutB:
    correct tree: PROBE failing-recipient: stillCached=false genDelta=2  -> PASS
                  PROBE working-recipient: stillCached=false genDelta=2  -> PASS
    mutB:         PROBE failing-recipient: stillCached=false genDelta=1  -> PASS
                  PROBE working-recipient: stillCached=false genDelta=2  -> PASS
  Cache CONTENTS are identical in every combination -- the helper's error
  branch (`failed = append(...); s.invalidateCache(path); continue`) evicts on
  the way out, so a state assertion made after the call cannot see the
  difference. Confirmed, not taken on trust.

  I then tested the one simpler discriminator the worker did NOT mention and I
  found myself: the private s.cacheGen counter, which differs 2 vs 1 on the
  failing-recipient path above. It is NOT a valid test of the property, and I
  proved that with a third mutation (mutE) that moves the invalidate to
  immediately AFTER encryptData but BEFORE the err check -- the plaintext is
  resident for the whole transform, the worst version of the bug:
    $ cd mutE && go test -count=1 -v -run 'TestProbe|TestRollbackDecryptionInvalidatesCacheBeforeReencrypt' ./internal/services/storage/
      --- FAIL: TestRollbackDecryptionInvalidatesCacheBeforeReencrypt
          migration_cache_test.go:553: plaintext still resident in cache while
          encryptData is in flight ...
      zz_probe_test.go:36: PROBE failing-recipient: ... genDelta=2  --- PASS
      zz_probe_test.go:72: PROBE working-recipient: ... genDelta=2  --- PASS
  genDelta=2 is exactly the correct tree's value, so a counter-based test would
  have passed a tree with the defect fully present. Only the blocking test
  catches it. The machinery is necessary; a post-hoc test of either kind would
  have been the same class of non-test I rejected at attempt 2.

POINT 2b -- soundness of the blocking test.
  Channel-synchronised, not timing-dependent: migration_cache_test.go:467-471
  `close(r.started); <-r.proceed` inside Wrap; the test waits on `<-br.started`
  (line 542) and releases with `close(br.proceed)` (556). The two 5s
  time.After arms are failure backstops only; neither carries a passing path.
  No deadlock when the assertion fails: line 552-554 is t.Errorf, not t.Fatal,
  so `close(br.proceed)` at 556 still runs. Demonstrated, not reasoned:
    $ cd mutB && timeout 120 go test -race -count=3 -v -run 'TestRollbackDecryptionInvalidatesCacheBeforeReencrypt' ./internal/services/storage/
      --- FAIL (0.00s) x3, each with the line-553 message
      FAIL  budget2/internal/services/storage  0.017s
    Fails 3/3, deterministically, in 17ms total -- it returns rather than
    hanging, and the 120s timeout did not fire.
  Safe under -race and repetition on the unmutated tree:
    $ cd base && go test -race -count=5 -v -run 'TestRollbackDecryptionInvalidatesCacheBeforeReencrypt' ./internal/services/storage/
      --- PASS x5, ok 1.024s, no WARNING: DATA RACE
    $ cd base && go test -race -count=2 -v -run 'TestRollbackDecryptionInvalidatesCacheBeforeReencrypt|TestDisableEncryptionPartialFailureNothingDecryptedYet' ./internal/services/storage/
      --- PASS x2 each, ok 12.056s
  The one construct worth checking is `entry.data` read at line 552 after
  RUnlock at 551: cache is map[string]*cacheEntry, and grep shows cacheEntry
  values are only ever constructed fresh (storage.go:339 publishCache(&cacheEntry{...}))
  and copied out (306-307), never mutated in place, so the pointer read is not
  a race -- and -race agrees over 5 runs. `close(r.started)` cannot double-close:
  one recipient means age calls Wrap once, and -count=5 makes fresh channels
  per run (no panic observed).

