KNOWN GAP -- still real, still honestly recorded, no vacuous test added.
  $ grep -rn "PLAINTEXT\|could NOT be re-encrypted\|address them manually" \
      internal/services/storage/*_test.go
    (no match -- nothing in the suite so much as mentions this branch)
  I reproduced the gap mutation myself (mutGap): the whole
  `if len(failed) > 0 { return fmt.Errorf(...PLAINTEXT ON DISK...) }` block
  replaced by `_ = s.rollbackDecryptionWithRecipient(...)`, so the call always
  claims a successful restore.
    $ cd mutGap && go test -count=1 ./internal/services/storage/
      ok  budget2/internal/services/storage  61.278s   (gap_suite_rc=0)
  The suite is green with the user-facing exposure warning deleted. This is
  the same gap I recorded at attempt 2 and it is unchanged. Critically, no test
  was added at attempt 3 that appears to cover it: the two new tests are named
  for, and assert, the guard branch and the cache placement respectively, and
  both are proven to fail when their own branch is broken (mutA, mutB). There
  is no test standing in front of this branch giving false assurance. The gap
  is a coverage hole with an honest label, which is the right disposition
  given that neither the worker nor I could construct an end-to-end fixture --
  DisableEncryption holds s.mu, and decrypt and re-encrypt of the same path go
  through the same directory, so in-process no file can be restorable at
  decrypt time and unrestorable at rollback time. I did not attempt the
  RLIMIT_FSIZE route; the worker's stated reason for rejecting it (the limit is
  process-wide and truncated its own test output) is correct on its face -- Go
  runs all tests in one process, so an RLIMIT_FSIZE low enough to break
  atomicWrite would also break the testing package's own writes.
  One process note, not scored: this reasoning lives only in verdict files and
  worker messages, not in any file in the repo. If F3 is accepted, the gap
  should be written into SPEC.md or a comment at migration.go:202, or the next
  person to touch this function will not know it is undefended.

